{
  "_comment": "Status cues (Documentation/design/graph-normalization.md#status). A `pre` cue stands at most `window` words before the entity name, a `post` cue at most `postWindow` words after it (`X was ruled out`), both in the same clause; a cue with its own `window` uses that instead. Bare negations (`no`, `ohne`) reach only the next word or two, so `no prior treatment has prostate cancer` negates nothing. Where two cues overlap, the longer wins: `cannot be ruled out` beats `ruled out`, `not confirmed` beats `confirmed`. `pseudo` cues only block shorter ones (`no change` is not a negation, `not confirmed` says neither yes nor no). So does a cue with one of `negators` among the `negatorWindow` words before it: `could not be ruled out` and `has not been confirmed` give no status (`t` is what normalizeName leaves of `can't`). Cues and clause breaks are matched after normalizeName.",
